f 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Problems Using Unit Rates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1033 558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050273 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3bc5deb9-7ddf-5f6d-86b1-c0f92239ac2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1031 "Speed as a Unit Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1033 3593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050943 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "73c67a70-178b-5e99-8871-46ff25f570ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073635 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073635 #edb/ref 1071 "If a machine at a factory takes $6$ hours to complete $2$ batches of refractory bricks, how many hours does it take to complete $10$ batches?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073635 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a86fdc21-1c98-5929-8282-aed055b1b053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073635 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches of refractory bricks, we first find the unit rate of hours per batch and then multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches.\n\nTo find the unit rate of hours per batch, we divide the number of hours, $6,$ by the number of batches, $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6÷2 & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $3$ hours per batch. To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches, we multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\times 10 & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the machine $30$ hours to complete $10$ batches." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073636 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073636 #edb/ref 1071 "At a certain sports center, there are $5$ equal-size pools. If a pump can empty $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ of a pool in $15$ minutes, then how many minutes will it take the pump to empty all $5$ pools?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073636 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52c7bc18-eedc-504f-bbae-a501944834a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073636 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15,$ by the total pool volume emptied, $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{2} & =15\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 2}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{3} \\\\  & =30÷3 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 5 & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073637 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073637 #edb/ref 1071 "Ava works in a coffee store. She earns a bonus of $\\dfrac 3 4$ of a dollar for each $\\dfrac 3 8$ liters of coffee sold. How much of a bonus will Ava earn if she sells $18$ liters of coffee?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073637 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f61ec9c-9b87-5b25-9543-44b6043b7b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073637 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of liters, $\\dfrac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{4\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =24÷12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 18 & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074400 #edb/ref 175921860