107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491107 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491107 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491107 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491107 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f90489e0-fda9-52b4-aa57-bb15b814888a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1071 "Farhan can buy $2$ slices of pizza for a dollar. If he has $14$ dollars, then how many slices of pizza can he buy?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "580104c9-cac0-5746-a4a5-7ac914d81f7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491147 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit rate is $2$ slices per dollar. To find the number of slices Farhan can buy for $14$ dollars, we multiply the unit rate by $14$ dollars and get\n\n$$\n2\\times 14=28.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Farhan can buy $28$ slices for $14$ dollars." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1071 "Sophie decides to go for a walk. If she walks $\\frac{3}{5}$ kilometers in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of an hour, how many kilometers does she walk in $2$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60a4c1ac-7be9-527c-8cb4-fa53e2f8804b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491150 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of kilometers that Sophie walks in $2$ hours, we can first find the unit rate of kilometers per hour and multiply the unit rate by $2$ hours.\n\nTo find the unit rate of kilometers per hour, we divide the number of kilometers, $\\frac{3}{5}$, by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{10}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{10}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 10}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =30÷5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $6$ kilometers per hour. To find the number of kilometers that Sophie walks in $2$ hours, we multiply the unit rate by $2$ hours and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\times 2 & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sophie walks $12$ kilometers in $2$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1071 "Laura can decorate $5$ cakes in $\\frac{5}{2}$ hours. How many cakes can she decorate in $3$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ee32ba5-d533-567a-a32c-16aed9424364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491159 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the number of cakes Laura can decorate in $3$ hours, we first find the unit rate of cakes per hour and then multiply it by $3$ hours.\n\nTo find the unit rate of cakes per hour, we divide the number of cakes, $5,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{5}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5÷\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷\\frac{5}{2} & =5\\times \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 2}{1\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =10÷5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ cakes per hour. To determine the number of cakes Laura can decorate in $3$ hours, we multiply the unit rate by $3$ hours and get\n\n$$\n2\\times 3=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Laura can decorate $6$ cakes in $3$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1071 "It takes Douglas $20$ minutes to shear $5$ sheep. How many minutes will it take to shear $9$ sheep?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "803aecb5-0c77-5ccb-b734-dbe320a2826b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491170 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the number of minutes it will take Douglas to shear $9$ sheep, we first find the unit rate of minutes per sheep and then multiply it by $9$ sheep.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per sheep, we divide the number of minutes, $20,$ by the number of sheep, $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20÷5 & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $4$ minutes per sheep. To find the number of minutes it takes Douglas to shear $9$ sheep, we multiply the unit rate by $9$ sheep and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\times 9 & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes Douglas $36$ minutes to shear $9$ sheep." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1071 "To make a cake mix, Eva uses $\\frac{3}{8}$ of a cup of wheat flour and $\\frac{3}{16}$ of a cup of sugar. How many cups of flour does she need if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec888cfc-fd30-59e8-bd79-e4af221c07b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491210 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the number of cups of flour Eva needs if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar, we first find the unit ratio of cups of flour per cup of sugar and then multiply it by $2$ cups of sugar.\n\nTo find the unit ratio of cups of flour per cup of sugar, we divide the number of cups of flour, $\\frac{3}{8},$ by the number of cups of sugar, $\\frac{3}{16},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{3}{16}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{3}{16} & =\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 16}{8\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{24} \\\\  & =48÷24 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ cups of flour per cup of sugar. To determine the number of cups of flour Eva needs if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar, we multiply the unit rate by $2$ cups of sugar and get\n\n$$\n2\\times 2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Eva needs $4$ cups of flour if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1071 "Marcel can transcribe $\\frac{3}{8}$ of a paper in $3$ hours. How many hours does it take him to transcribe $5$ papers?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f450169b-fcda-5464-b467-6f84236b6273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491214 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of hours Marcel takes to transcribe $5$ papers, we can first find the unit rate of hours per paper, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ papers.\n\nTo find the unit rate of hours per paper, we divide the number of hours, $3,$ by the number of papers, $\\frac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{3}{8} & =3\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{3} \\\\  & =24÷3 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $8$ hours per paper. To find the number of hours that Marcel takes to transcribe $5$ papers, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ papers and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\times 5 & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Marcel can transcribe $5$ papers in $40$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
